autosave_2024-03-30_15.27.41.vgd: 1671 object(s), Conversion finished with 20 note(s):&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat's two arrows are the only shapes with no equivalent here, so they were added to the level's own shape resources. (x2, first at prefabs[0].objs[0])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's object origin was converted into this format's pivot; the two measure the same idea from opposite sides, so check objects whose origin was not centred. (x344, first at prefabs[0].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat keeps opacity separately from the theme colour, which this format's theme reference cannot. Semi-transparent colours were resolved to literals and no longer follow a theme change. (x428, first at prefabs[0].objs[2])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat prefabs carry a preview image; this format has no field for one and it is not imported. (x10, first at prefabs[0])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat randomizes a rotation as a delta from the keyframe before it, so its spread grows along a track; this format rolls every keyframe against the authored total, so those tracks vary by the right amount at each keyframe but no longer drift apart along it. (x2, first at prefabs[2].objs[3])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Some objects name a parent that is not in the level; those objects were imported as roots. (x9, first at prefabs[4].objs[11])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Some objects have children that disagree about inheriting their scale, and their own scale is animated; this format cannot express both at once, so the disagreeing children follow the majority. (x9, first at prefabs[5].objs)&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat skews a rotated object sitting under a non-uniformly scaled parent; this format has no skew to give, so the object is fitted to the nearest rotation and scale it can hold. The wider apart the parent's two scales are, the more of the skew is lost. (x130, first at prefabs[7].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Bounce easing has no equivalent here; those keyframes use Elastic, which is the closest motion available. (x17579, first at prefabs[7].objs[6])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat can stop a child following its parent's rotation while it still follows the rest; this format inherits the whole transform, so those objects turn with their parent here. (x4, first at objects[185])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's background depth of field has no equivalent here and is not imported. (parallax_settings)&#xD;
  [Info] Prefab placements were imported as placements rather than as copies of their templates, with the id table that makes their content rebuildable. (prefab_objects)&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat's background subsystem has no equivalent here; the theme's own background colour was placed on the camera so the level keeps the colour behind it. ()&#xD;
  [Info] The player is scaled for this level so it matches the size Afterbeat's own player is against the same content; change the first keyframe of the player size track to taste. ()&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat only ever runs at 16:9, so the level was authored for that frame and is pinned to it. Without the pin a wider screen would show what its author left outside it. ()&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's film grain carries a luminance response; this format's carries a preset and an intensity, so the response was not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's single glitch effect became this format's analog and digital pair; its width and speed have no equivalent. (events)&#xD;
  [Deferred] Afterbeat's player force track was imported, but this format's player does not apply it yet - the level plays as if the track were not there. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's full-screen gradient overlay has no equivalent here and is not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Some level-global tracks carry more keyframes than this format allows; the ones past the limit were dropped, so those effects stop changing before the end of the level. (events)&#xD;
&#xD;
