  [Info] Afterbeat's background subsystem has no equivalent here; the theme's own background colour was placed on the camera so the level keeps the colour behind it. ()&#xD;
  [Info] The player is scaled for this level so it matches the size Afterbeat's own player is against the same content; change the first keyframe of the player size track to taste. ()&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat only ever runs at 16:9, so the level was authored for that frame and is pinned to it. Without the pin a wider screen would show what its author left outside it. ()&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's film grain carries a luminance response; this format's carries a preset and an intensity, so the response was not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's single glitch effect became this format's analog and digital pair; its width and speed have no equivalent. (events)&#xD;
  [Deferred] Afterbeat's player force track was imported, but this format's player does not apply it yet - the level plays as if the track were not there. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's full-screen gradient overlay has no equivalent here and is not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's triggers (visual novel, player control, time control) have no equivalent here and are not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Some markers cover a stretch of the level rather than one moment; this format's markers are points, so those land on their start and their length is not imported. (x5, first at events)&#xD;
&#xD;
autosave_2026-06-20_01.03.01.vgd: 12771 object(s), Conversion finished with 48 note(s):&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's object origin was converted into this format's pivot; the two measure the same idea from opposite sides, so check objects whose origin was not centred. (x2360, first at prefabs[0].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat keeps opacity separately from the theme colour, which this format's theme reference cannot. Semi-transparent colours were resolved to literals and no longer follow a theme change. (x7134, first at prefabs[0].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat prefabs carry a preview image; this format has no field for one and it is not imported. (x19, first at prefabs[0])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat skews a rotated object sitting under a non-uniformly scaled parent; this format has no skew to give, so the object is fitted to the nearest rotation and scale it can hold. The wider apart the parent's two scales are, the more of the skew is lost. (x2868, first at prefabs[6].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Some objects name a parent that is not in the level; those objects were imported as roots. (x3, first at prefabs[6].objs[6])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat can stop a child following its parent's rotation while it still follows the rest; this format inherits the whole transform, so those objects turn with their parent here. (x334, first at prefabs[6].objs[6])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat text carries inline formatting tags; the ones this format shares with TextMeshPro are interpreted, the rest are drawn as literal text. (x378, first at prefabs[6].objs[7])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat text has no bounds; imported text was given a block one character wide per character and one line tall per line, which fits any typeface rather than matching the source exactly. (x447, first at prefabs[6].objs[7])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Some prefabs contain objects that die at a fixed moment of the SONG and are placed at several different times; every copy was resolved against the earliest of those placements, so the later ones stay on screen longer than they did. (x2, first at prefabs[8])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Some keyframes landed on the same frame once converted from seconds; the later one was kept. A higher framerate keeps them apart. (x14, first at prefabs[8].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat can delay a child's inheritance from its parent in time; this format has no equivalent, so those delays are not imported. (x168, first at prefabs[9].objs[0])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's Scale randomization multiplies both axes of a point by the same factor; this format rolls each axis on its own, so the two are no longer locked to each other. (x905, first at prefabs[9].objs[4])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Objects only hurt the player at full opacity in the source game; a faded object was imported as a collider-less object plus one invisible collider per fully opaque stretch. (x215, first at prefabs[9].objs[34])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat randomizes a rotation as a delta from the keyframe before it, so its spread grows along a track; this format rolls every keyframe against the authored total, so those tracks vary by the right amount at each keyframe but no longer drift apart along it. (x1117, first at prefabs[9].objs[41])&#xD;
  [Info] Custom polygons whose corners are rounded have no built-in equivalent here, so each distinct one became a shape resource. (x131, first at prefabs[9].objs[52])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Some objects have children that disagree about inheriting their scale, and their own scale is animated; this format cannot express both at once, so the disagreeing children follow the majority. (x88, first at prefabs[10].objs)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's radial object gradient is a shape this format's four-corner colour cannot hold; those objects arrive filled flat with the colour their edge had. (x84, first at prefabs[13].objs[23])&#xD;
  [Approximated] A gradient corner falling between two theme colours is a blend, which no theme reference can express; those corners were baked into literal colours and no longer follow a theme change. (x21, first at prefabs[13].objs[23])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Objects that never reach full opacity cannot hurt the player in the source game, so they were imported without a collider. (x203, first at prefabs[15].objs[1])&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat scales everything parented to its camera by the camera's own zoom, so a node carrying that scale was rebuilt and those objects hang off it - without it they draw at the size they were authored at rather than the size they were seen at. (objects)&#xD;
  [Approximated] Bounce easing has no equivalent here; those keyframes use Elastic, which is the closest motion available. (x54, first at objects[18])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat sets a text's typeface inline with TextMeshPro's &lt;font&gt; tag; a font here is a property of the object, so the tags were removed and the object was given the closest of this game's own typefaces. (x90, first at objects[59])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Some text objects carry more than 1024 characters, which is the fixed length this format's text buffers hold; those strings were cut to fit. (x23, first at objects[444])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat rotates individual glyphs with TextMeshPro's &lt;rotate&gt; tag, which has no counterpart here; the tags were removed and the glyphs stand upright. (x27, first at objects[1879])&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat's two arrows are the only shapes with no equivalent here, so they were added to the level's own shape resources. (objects[2419])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's Toggle randomization flips both axes of a point together; this format rolls each axis on its own, so those keyframes can also land on the two mixed corners. (x37, first at objects[5590])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat lays text out by one of three alignments rather than by a pivot; an origin between them is centred there and is placed proportionally here. (x6, first at objects[5695])&#xD;
  [Approximated] A gradient that reaches its end colours before the edge of its object cannot be said with four corners; those gradients arrive spread more gently across the object than they were authored. (x339, first at objects[5886])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat particle emitters were imported as effects: the emission rate, the particle mesh, its lifetime and the emitter volume cross, while world-space emission, the per-particle velocity curve and an animated emitter volume have no counterpart here. (x418, first at objects[8198])&#xD;
  [Approximated] A world-space Afterbeat emitter leaves its particles behind as it travels; effects here always simulate in their own space, so those particles are dragged along with the emitter instead. (x409, first at objects[8198])&#xD;
  [Approximated] A gradient stop here holds a literal colour rather than a theme slot, so imported particle colours were resolved against the level's own theme once and no longer follow a theme change. (x418, first at objects[8198])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat animates where a particle travels over its whole life; only one start velocity crosses here, so that travel is flattened to its average. (x12, first at objects[8568])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat can emit a particle every so many units travelled; there is no distance-based emission here, so those emitters spawn on their time rate alone. (x11, first at objects[8623])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat can animate the volume particles spawn inside; an emitter shape here is a value rather than a track, so only its first keyframe crosses. (x6, first at objects[8623])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat pushes a particle along its emitter shape's normal at birth; there is no radial-outward force here, so that push is lost. (x2, first at objects[8655])&#xD;
  [Approximated] Shape 'Custom 32-gon Outline 1/32' could not be built; those objects use a Square. (objects[9829])&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's background depth of field has no equivalent here and is not imported. (parallax_settings)&#xD;
  [Info] Prefab placements were imported as placements rather than as copies of their templates, with the id table that makes their content rebuildable. (prefab_objects)&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat blends between two fixed shake generators, which is not a rate; those keyframes shake at the matching point of this engine's own rate range instead. (x207, first at events)&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat's background subsystem has no equivalent here; the theme's own background colour was placed on the camera so the level keeps the colour behind it. ()&#xD;
  [Info] The player is scaled for this level so it matches the size Afterbeat's own player is against the same content; change the first keyframe of the player size track to taste. ()&#xD;
  [Info] Afterbeat only ever runs at 16:9, so the level was authored for that frame and is pinned to it. Without the pin a wider screen would show what its author left outside it. ()&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's film grain carries a luminance response; this format's carries a preset and an intensity, so the response was not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Approximated] Afterbeat's single glitch effect became this format's analog and digital pair; its width and speed have no equivalent. (events)&#xD;
  [Deferred] Afterbeat's player force track was imported, but this format's player does not apply it yet - the level plays as if the track were not there. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's full-screen gradient overlay has no equivalent here and is not imported. (events)&#xD;
  [Dropped] Afterbeat's triggers (visual novel, player control, time control) have no equivalent here and are not imported. (events)&#xD;
